<GuideLayout
  title="Your first overnight hike: what you actually need"
  description="A practical checklist that separates genuine essentials from gear you can add once you know what kind of hiking you enjoy."
  eyebrow="Start here"
  readTime="7 min read"
>
  <h2>Build around four core jobs</h2><p>
    Your overnight kit needs to shelter you, keep you warm, carry your load and
    help you eat and drink. Start there. The specialist gadgets can wait.
  </p>
  <h3>1. Shelter</h3><p>
    Choose a tent or shelter that suits the expected wind, rain, insects and
    campsite surface. For a first trip, an uncomplicated two-person tent can be
    worth a little extra weight because it gives a solo hiker useful room and is
    often easier to live with.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Sleep system</h3><p>
    Your sleeping bag and mat work together. A warm bag cannot stop the ground
    drawing heat from below, while a warm mat cannot compensate for a bag that
    is inadequate for the overnight low.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Pack</h3><p>
    Buy the pack after you understand the volume of the rest of your kit. Around
    45-55 litres is a common starting range for a compact overnight setup, but
    fit matters more than a number on a label.
  </p>
  <h3>4. Food and water</h3><p>
    For one night, cooking can be very simple. Plan your water sources and
    treatment method before leaving, and carry enough capacity for dry sections.
  </p>
  <h2>The essential checklist</h2><ul>
    <li>
      Shelter, poles or trekking-pole support, pegs and a groundsheet if
      required
    </li><li>Sleeping bag or quilt suited to the expected low</li><li>
      Sleeping mat with suitable insulation
    </li><li>Pack that fits your torso and carries the load comfortably</li><li>
      Weather-appropriate layers and rain protection
    </li><li>Navigation you know how to use, plus a backup</li><li>
      Headlamp, first aid, sun protection, repair items and emergency
      communication
    </li><li>Food, water capacity and a treatment method where needed</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Do a low-consequence first trip</h2><p>
    Choose a short route with a clear forecast, an established campsite and an
    easy exit. Set up every piece of equipment at home first. A backyard or
    nearby campground test is useful because the fastest way to learn a new
    shelter is before the weather turns.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
